       The only difference between pipes and FIFOs is the manner in which they
       are created and opened.  Once these tasks have been  accomplished,  I/O
       on pipes and FIFOs has exactly the same semantics.

       If  a  process  attempts  to read from an empty pipe, then rreeaadd(2) will
       block until data is available.  If a process attempts  to  write  to  a
       full  pipe  (see below), then wwrriittee(2) blocks until sufficient data has
       been read from the pipe to allow the write to complete.

       Nonblocking I/O is possible by using the ffccnnttll(2) FF__SSEETTFFLL operation  to
       enable  the  OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK  open  file status flag or by opening a ffiiffoo(7)
       with OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK.  If any process has the pipe open for  writing,  reads
       fail with EEAAGGAAIINN; otherwise—with no potential writers—reads succeed and
       return empty.

       The communication channel provided by a pipe is a _b_y_t_e _s_t_r_e_a_m: there is
       no concept of message boundaries.

       If  all file descriptors referring to the write end of a pipe have been
       closed, then an attempt to rreeaadd(2) from the pipe will  see  end‐of‐file
       (rreeaadd(2) will return 0).  If all file descriptors referring to the read
       end  of  a  pipe have been closed, then a wwrriittee(2) will cause a SSIIGGPPIIPPEE
       signal to be generated for the calling process.  If the calling process
       is ignoring this signal, then wwrriittee(2) fails with the error EEPPIIPPEE.   An
       application  that uses ppiippee(2) and ffoorrkk(2) should use suitable cclloossee(2)
       calls to close unnecessary duplicate  file  descriptors;  this  ensures
       that end‐of‐file and SSIIGGPPIIPPEE/EEPPIIPPEE are delivered when appropriate.

       It is not possible to apply llsseeeekk(2) to a pipe.

