       A  pipe  has  a limited capacity.  If the pipe is full, then a wwrriittee(2)
       will block or fail, depending on whether the  OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK  flag  is  set
       (see  below).   Different implementations have different limits for the
       pipe capacity.  Applications should not rely on a particular  capacity:
       an  application  should  be designed so that a reading process consumes
       data as soon as it is available, so that a writing process does not re‐
       main blocked.

       Before Linux 2.6.11, the capacity of a pipe was the same as the  system
       page size (e.g., 4096 bytes on i386).  Since Linux 2.6.11, the pipe ca‐
       pacity  is 16 pages (i.e., 65,536 bytes in a system with a page size of
       4096 bytes).  Since Linux 2.6.35,  the  default  pipe  capacity  is  16
       pages,  but  the  capacity  can  be  queried and set using the ffccnnttll(2)
       FF__GGEETTPPIIPPEE__SSZZ and FF__SSEETTPPIIPPEE__SSZZ operations.  See ffccnnttll(2) for more infor‐
       mation.  Since Linux 4.5, the default pipe capacity is  lower  than  16
       pages when the _p_i_p_e_-_u_s_e_r_-_p_a_g_e_s_-_s_o_f_t limit is exceeded.

       The  following  iiooccttll(2)  operation, which can be applied to a file de‐
       scriptor that refers to either end of a pipe, places  a  count  of  the
       number  of unread bytes in the pipe in the _i_n_t buffer pointed to by the
       final argument of the call:

           ioctl(fd, FIONREAD, &nbytes);

       The FFIIOONNRREEAADD operation is not specified in any standard,  but  is  pro‐
       vided on many implementations.

