   PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF
       POSIX.1 says that writes of less than PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF bytes  must  be  atomic:
       the  output  data  is  written  to  the  pipe as a contiguous sequence.
       Writes of more than PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF bytes may be nonatomic: the kernel may in‐
       terleave the data with data written by other  processes.   POSIX.1  re‐
       quires  PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF to be at least 512 bytes.  (On Linux, PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF is 4096
       bytes.)  The precise semantics depend on whether the file descriptor is
       nonblocking (OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK), whether there are  multiple  writers  to  the
       pipe, and on _n, the number of bytes to be written:

       OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK disabled, _n <= PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF
              All  _n bytes are written atomically; wwrriittee(2) may block if there
              is not room for _n bytes to be written immediately

       OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK enabled, _n <= PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF
              If there is room to write _n bytes to  the  pipe,  then  wwrriittee(2)
              succeeds  immediately,  writing  all _n bytes; otherwise wwrriittee(2)
              fails, with _e_r_r_n_o set to EEAAGGAAIINN.

       OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK disabled, _n > PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF
              The write is nonatomic: the data given to wwrriittee(2) may be inter‐
              leaved with wwrriittee(2)s by other process; the wwrriittee(2) blocks  un‐
              til _n bytes have been written.

       OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK enabled, _n > PPIIPPEE__BBUUFF
              If  the pipe is full, then wwrriittee(2) fails, with _e_r_r_n_o set to EEAA‐‐
              GGAAIINN.  Otherwise, from 1 to _n bytes  may  be  written  (i.e.,  a
              "partial  write"  may  occur; the caller should check the return
              value from wwrriittee(2) to see how many bytes  were  actually  writ‐
              ten),  and  these  bytes may be interleaved with writes by other
              processes.

